comments.body:
@participant1  `wasm2mpy` should be easy to get started if you're on linux:
https://github.com/participant2/wasm2mpy

I'd be happy to guide you if needed

@participant2 just for the fun of it, can you please check if the attached mpy file works on armv6m?

I noticed that the soft-fp object files I had in my gcc for arm bare metal toolchian (14.1.0) do not have any of those absolute relocations so I tried to build using those files and no further fixes to mpy_ld.py and it built just fine...

[coremark.mpy.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/16855502/coremark.mpy.zip)

@participant2 colour me surprised, that patch wasn't supposed to work :)

Anyway, I got an RP2040 and I tried building the coremark mpy.  It imports without errors but doesn't do anything.  Is there any particular function I need to call on the object?

On both the pre-built mpy file in your sin/cos test and on the coremark mpy I built, invoking `dir()` on the imported module object is broken - it works on other platforms.

@participant1 thanks for the very quick answer. It sounds like armv6m support for soft-float would require a considerable amount of work in the mpy_ld linker, and that it is not really a "bug" per now that it does not work. One question: Do you think it would be possible to convince GCC to output relative relocations for armv6m - same as for the other architectures? Perhaps that could be a path to having this be supported?
The one GCC version I got from Arch Linux packages did not hit this (though I do not know why).

And considering that this is a known limitation, maybe we should put it a bit more user-friendly error message in mpy_ld.py for this case? Right now, it just hits a catch-all AssertionError.
